import{_ as a,d as t,e as i,y as n}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Allocation can shape the capability used to justify later allocation","description":"A reinforcing loop requires evidence across rounds, not just a repeated claim of merit.","frontmatter":{"title":"Allocation can shape the capability used to justify later allocation","description":"A reinforcing loop requires evidence across rounds, not just a repeated claim of merit."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/concepts/allocation-and-capability.md","filePath":"en/concepts/allocation-and-capability.md"}'),o={name:"en/concepts/allocation-and-capability.md"};function r(s,e,l,c,p,d){return t(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="allocation-can-shape-the-capability-used-to-justify-later-allocation" tabindex="-1">Allocation can shape the capability used to justify later allocation <a class="header-anchor" href="#allocation-can-shape-the-capability-used-to-justify-later-allocation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Allocation can shape the capability used to justify later allocation&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>A reinforcing loop requires evidence across rounds, not just a repeated claim of merit.</strong></p><p>An allocation of practice, opportunity, or responsibility can help form capability. Later allocation may then use that capability as a criterion, widening a gap or concentrating opportunities. This is a candidate mechanism with multiple links and at least two rounds to examine.</p><p>Check whether the relevant capability actually depends on practice and whether that practice is obtained mainly through the allocation in question. Then check whether current capability actually changes the next allocation. Prior differences, incentives, training duration, legal conditions, preferences, attrition, scarcity, discrimination, trust, and incumbents’ interests may provide competing or interacting explanations.</p><p>The gap or concentration must widen relative to the starting point, and capability formation, retention, and later use need evidence under §4.5. If material is insufficient, retain a candidate. Formal mechanism support additionally requires C10’s carrier-reproduction route; repeated association alone does not qualify.</p><p>The capability difference can be real even if its formation was unequal. Recognizing its reality does not establish fair opportunity or dictate a future allocation rule. Conversely, unequal allocation among equally capable people may challenge the proposed capability-to-allocation link and require a different mechanism.</p><p>Discriminating observations compare when the gap began, whether equally capable people receive comparable allocations, and whether predeclared, independently verifiable handover, training, or access conditions reduce the gap within a window appropriate to capability formation. Compare units with similarly tight resources but different allocation rules. Contrary observations require narrowing or withdrawing the affected link, not changing the meaning of genuine support after the outcome.</p><h2 id="keep-these-judgments-separate" tabindex="-1">Keep these judgments separate <a class="header-anchor" href="#keep-these-judgments-separate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Keep these judgments separate&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Measured capability</th><th>Fair allocation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A task-specific difference that may have evidence.</td><td>A normative judgment about opportunities, criteria, burdens, and alternatives.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="teaching-example-practice-opportunities-across-two-rounds" tabindex="-1">Teaching example: practice opportunities across two rounds <a class="header-anchor" href="#teaching-example-practice-opportunities-across-two-rounds" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Teaching example: practice opportunities across two rounds&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Suppose group A receives practice, improves on a relevant task, and that measured improvement is used to assign the next practice round. Trace each link and compare competing explanations before calling this reinforcement.</p><p><strong>Assumptions:</strong> Constructed mechanism candidate, not an observation of a real group.</p><p><strong>Boundary:</strong> The pattern does not by itself prove malicious intent or justify equal or unequal allocation.</p><h2 id="check-your-reading" tabindex="-1">Check your reading <a class="header-anchor" href="#check-your-reading" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Check your reading&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Does a manager repeatedly saying “capability” establish the second link?</strong></p><p>No. The criterion must actually affect the later allocation; rhetoric alone does not identify the mechanism.</p><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p>A loop cannot be established from one static association.</p></li><li><p>Capability, opportunity, authority, and normative justification require distinct evidence.</p></li><li><p>Support or training conditions used in a comparison must be stated beforehand and independently verifiable. A lack of improvement cannot be excused afterward by asserting that the offered conditions were not genuine.</p></li></ul><p>Read the complete <a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#section-7-6-1">source section §7.6.1</a> and its guide. This explanation follows theory v2; the <a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/downloads.html">Chinese original</a> remains authoritative.</p>',19)])])}const m=a(o,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
